    "body": "BMW opened its $700 million battery assembly plant in Woodruff, South Carolina last week and began building battery packs for the model iX5. The packs will travel 15 miles to BMW’s Spartanburg plant, where they’ll go into the first fully electric BMW built in the United States.\nWhat’s Related\nBMW plans to start assembling the iX5 in Spartanburg before the end of 2026 and put it on sale in the first quarter of 2027. The company expects to build at least six fully electric models at Spartanburg by 2030, with Woodruff supplying battery packs for future models as well.\nThe Woodruff plant is part of a $1.7 billion South Carolina investment BMW announced in 2022. The company put the other $1 billion into preparing its Spartanburg plant to build electric vehicles . Woodruff began regular battery pack production in November, ahead of the December start BMW had projected in July.\n“With today’s opening of Plant Woodruff, we demonstrate how quickly we can scale a new battery concept to industrial production,” said Rich Everly, BMW’s vice president responsible for manufacturing at the Woodruff plant. Construction began in September 2023, equipment installation started a year later while crews were still working on the building, and the first test packs came off the line in summer 2025.\nHow Woodruff builds the packs\nWoodruff buys cylindrical battery cells from outside suppliers and checks their voltage when they arrive. The cells go directly into the pack, eliminating the step of building separate battery modules. BMW says that design saves weight. The pack in the iX5 60 xDrive holds 144 kilowatt-hours of usable energy, the most of any fully electric BMW battery to date.\nOn the line, groups of cells are attached to coolers before they’re laser-cleaned and welded. Equipment checks each weld as it’s made, and foam holds the components in place inside the pack. After the housing is sealed, BMW installs the battery’s main control unit and inspects every finished pack before it leaves for Spartanburg.\nHow workers are being trained\nBMW says more than 300 employees and about 250 robots work at Woodruff. Production employees practice on a virtual copy of the assembly line before using the equipment. BMW also used computer models of the plant to plan production before the line was ready to run.\nDuring production, cameras and sensors collect data as the packs move down the line. BMW’s software analyzes that data and gives employees feedback while they work. The company uses the same system to check vehicle assembly at Spartanburg.\nWhere BMW gets its battery cells\nBMW did not name the supplier for Woodruff’s first production packs. Four days before the opening, the automaker announced a deal with LG Energy Solution to buy cells made at existing U.S. plants starting in the late 2020s. BMW also signed Samsung SDI as an additional supplier of cells made in Europe.\n“Diversifying procurement is an integral part of the BMW Group’s purchasing strategy,” said Nicolai Martin, the BMW board member responsible for purchasing. In Europe, the Samsung SDI deal will give BMW three suppliers making cells in the region, alongside CATL and EVE Energy.\nWoodruff is the fourth plant to begin building BMW’s sixth-generation battery packs. Debrecen, Hungary, started in October 2025, followed by plants in Germany and China in October 2026. A fifth site in San Luis Potosí, Mexico , is due to start in 2027. BMW placed each pack plant close to a vehicle factory to shorten the trip from battery assembly to installation.",
